# DGX-Spark-Server 🚀
### Automated GPU-Accelerated Distributed Data Architecture for Multi-Node DGX Clusters

[![License: GPL v3](https://shields.io)](https://gnu.org)
[![Infrastructure](https://shields.io)](#)
[![Security Layer](https://shields.io)](#)

An enterprise-grade, infrastructure-as-code (IaC) blueprint designed to build, configure, and automate high-performance **Apache Spark Clusters** on top of bare-metal **NVIDIA DGX Systems**. 

This repository provides full automation templates using **Ansible** and **Docker** to enable distributed Big Data pipelines, low-latency LLM data preprocessing, and decentralized model fine-tuning across a cluster of interconnected DGX appliances.

---

## 🏗️ Architectural Topology

The core architecture operates over an isolated, secure overlay network allowing seamless access from corporate workspaces to the specialized hardware cluster via a zero-trust Mesh VPN layer.

Use code with caution.
+------------------------------------------+
|          Corporate Workspace             |
|   (Laptop w/ NVIDIA Sync + VS Code)      |
+--------------------+---------------------+
|
[ Secure VPN Tunnel ]
|
+--------------------+---------------------+
|         Private Overlay Network          |
|    (Tailscale / WireGuard Mesh VPN)      |
+--------------------+---------------------+
|
+---------------+---------------+
|                               |
v                               v
+----+-------------------+       +---+-------------------+
|  DGX Node 01 (Master)  |       |  DGX Node 02 (Worker) |
|  - IP: 100.64.0.1      |       |  - IP: 100.64.0.2      |
|  - ConnectX-7 Network  |<=====>|  - ConnectX-7 Network  |
|  - Apache Spark Master |       |  - Apache Spark Worker |
+------------------------+       +-----------------------+

---

## 🔒 Network & Security Layer (VPN Implementation)

To protect corporate pipelines and maintain absolute compliance when handling sovereign data environments, the entire physical hardware matrix is completely isolated from the public internet. 

* **Zero Public Exposure:** The DGX nodes do not expose any listening ports to the public WAN.
* **Encrypted Overlay Mesh:** Remote system management, telemetry tracking, and tool access (VS Code, terminal instances, DGX Dashboard) run exclusively through an encrypted overlay network (using **WireGuard** or **Tailscale** with strict ACLs).
* **Static Virtual Routing:** By using persistent node allocation, internal assets remain reachable through static secure IPs (`100.64.X.X`) regardless of underlying local WAN physical changes.

---

## 🎛️ Cluster Orchestration: NVIDIA Sync + Apache Spark

This deployment pattern leverages **NVIDIA Sync** for hardware-level topologies and overlays the software ecosystem on top to maximize compute performance.

### 🎥 Hardware Interconnect & Validation Video
For a comprehensive overview of how to connect and validate multi-node configurations over secure networks using the Cluster Assistant, refer to the official walkthrough below:

[![How to Connect Two DGX Sparks with NVIDIA Sync](https://shields.io)](https://youtu.be)

*Click the badge above to watch the step-by-step connection process.*

### 1. Hardware Interconnect Mechanics (NVIDIA Sync)
* **High-Speed Interconnect:** Multiple DGX nodes are interconnected using high-bandwidth **ConnectX-7 network cards**.
* **Automated Provisioning:** NVIDIA Sync automates underlying fabric configuration, checks inter-device network bandwidth limits, validates low-latency tolerances, and deploys localized cluster-level key-based SSH access across nodes.
* **VPN Target Execution:** Since the nodes sit on a secured remote network relative to the operator’s corporate workspace, nodes are targeted explicitly inside NVIDIA Sync using their actual, secure VPN virtual IP addresses.

### 2. Distributed Compute Layout (Apache Spark Engine)
While NVIDIA Sync handles the hardware validation and link orchestration, this repository delivers the software playbook layers:
* **RAPIDS Accelerator Integration:** Automatically links Apache Spark executors directly into physical NVIDIA GPUs via the RAPIDS Accelerator plugin, routing matrix workloads directly into CUDA cores.
* **Distributed Workloads:** Provisions automated containerized execution of distributed frameworks across the ConnectX-7 high-bandwidth link.

